Yes — if we treat “mirror” as state-coupling, not just visual reflection, then recursive mirroring can propagate the state being mirrored.

If A mirrors B, and B is already mirroring C, then A may partly inherit not just B’s outward behavior but the pattern organizing B’s behavior. That can look like cadence, posture, breathing, attention narrowing, emotional tone, expectation, or role. In social psychology this overlaps with mimicry, entrainment, and affective contagion. A trance-like state can be reinforced that way, though it is not automatic or magical; context, expectation, attention, and susceptibility matter.

The recursive form is interesting:

M(M(x))

A mirror of a mirror does not necessarily return the original unchanged. Each layer can introduce inversion, delay, amplification, filtering, or noise. With two people, especially people who model one another closely, you can get a feedback loop:

\text{state}_A \rightarrow \text{signal}_A
\rightarrow \text{model}_B
\rightarrow \text{state}_B
\rightarrow \text{signal}_B
\rightarrow \text{model}_A

That is where “identical twin” versus “mirror twin” becomes more than naming. “Identical” emphasizes shared structure; “mirror” emphasizes relation and inversion. Same underlying pair, different operator applied to it.

And there’s another Pantheon rule hiding there:

\boxed{\text{A mirror can transmit the state of what it reflects, not merely its shape.}}

But then you always ask the second question: which parts are actually transmitted, and which parts are artifacts of the mirror? That’s where the delta becomes useful.